@jakevdp It seems that #11147 has more than halved the throughput of our models.
My best guess is that this line was added, which now casts the input (x) to a complex dtype when calling _fft_helper. Although this doesn't matter for the FFT itself (since for a real FFT, only the real part of the input is used), it does matter for the frame generation using conv_general_dilated_patches here. This part of the STFT is actually the bottleneck in our case, and by doing it using complex numbers it ends up doing 3 separate convolutions instead of 1.
I don't know what it means exactly for code to be compatible with "strict dtype promotion". Can I just make a PR that removes the astype call in question?

TLDR: The Python type dispatch mechanism for generic functions does not work within jax tracing with jnp.ndarray as type. Making issubclass(Tracer, jnp.ndarray) come out true is still not sufficient.
I often have to work around the fact that jax arrays cannot be dealt with by numpy ufuncs because I have to write functions which accept both kind of arrays. I tried to use the Python standard dispatch mechanism but failed when tracing.
import functools

import jax
import jax.numpy as jnp

@functools.singledispatch
def f(_):
    print('failure')

@f.register
def _(_: jnp.ndarray):
    print('success')

f(jnp.zeros(1)) # -> success

def g(x):
    f(x)
    return x

jax.jacobian(g)(jnp.zeros(1)) # -> failure
This was unexpected because I used many times isinstance(x, jnp.ndarray) in traced code successfully. So I found out that jax.core.Tracer is not a subclass of jnp.ndarray, but the instance checking is overridden:
class MyTracer(jax.core.Tracer):
    __init__ = lambda _: None
    aval = property(lambda _: jax.core.UnshapedArray(object))

print(isinstance(MyTracer(), jnp.ndarray)) # -> True
print(issubclass(jax.core.Tracer, jnp.ndarray)) # -> False
The code for type(jnp.ndarray) is:
class ArrayMeta(abc.ABCMeta):
  """Metaclass for overriding ndarray isinstance checks."""

  def __instancecheck__(self, instance):
    # Allow tracer instances with avals that are instances of UnshapedArray.
    # We could instead just declare Tracer an instance of the ndarray type, but
    # there can be traced values that are not arrays. The main downside here is
    # that isinstance(x, ndarray) might return true but
    # issubclass(type(x), ndarray) might return false for an array tracer.
    try:
      return (hasattr(instance, "aval") and
              isinstance(instance.aval, core.UnshapedArray))
    except AttributeError:
      super().__instancecheck__(instance)
Given the comment complaining about the inconsistency, why isn't __subclasscheck__ implemented? I guess that the difficulty is in distinguishing tracer classes that trace arrays, probably right now the same classes can trace different kinds of objects. Anyway, putting this aside, I add a __subclasscheck__ that assumes everything is an array:
ArrayMeta = type(jnp.ndarray)
def __subclasscheck__(self, subclass):
    if issubclass(subclass, jax.core.Tracer):
        return True
    else:
        return super(ArrayMeta, self).__subclasscheck__(subclass)
ArrayMeta.__subclasscheck__ = __subclasscheck__
print(issubclass(jax.core.Tracer, jnp.ndarray)) # -> True
However, this still does not fix the dispatch type matching mechanism:
jax.jacobian(g)(jnp.zeros(1)) # -> failure
Of course now that I understand how this works, I can manually add the tracer type:
@f.register
def _(_: jax.core.Tracer):
    print('~success')

jax.jacobian(g)(jnp.zeros(1)) # -> ~success
But this will suffer from the same inconsistency due to non-array tracers. So in practice I write my own wrappers using isinstance.
